  "summary": "Preprints and one paper offer latent-structure frameworks spanning cross-dataset alignment, digital twins, hyperbolic dynamics, plasticity clocks and subicular coding.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the recovery of latent structure from heterogeneous neural activity, though the items differ in scale and approach. Neuralign aligns and decomposes heterogeneous recordings into shared tensor components. A digital twin of freely moving mouse V1 is reported to recover classical tuning and behavioral gain effects, linking controlled physiology with active vision. NeuroMASS v4 applies learned hyperbolic latent geometry with state space models to Neural Latents Benchmark spike data and reports curvature differences. In Drosophila, heterogeneous short-term plasticity across Kenyon cell inputs is described as reformatting temporal information into an explicit population clock. The Disco theory frames subicular neurons as signaling discontinuities in a vector-coded experience, offering a unifying account of boundary, corner and axis-tuned cells. The common emphasis is on unifying frameworks, with heterogeneity treated as a resource.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
